WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.110
ABBY CAREY: Hey, I'm Abby.

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:04.830
And this is a look at Cloud
Code support for custom samples.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.660
Enterprise application
development

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.110
poses unique challenges when
juggling multiple organization

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.240
requirements and dependencies.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.310
But what if you had a
central starting point

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.760
to build applications configured
to your organization's needs?

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.030
That's where Cloud Code's custom
samples feature can help out.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:24.720
Custom samples can be starter
applications, services,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.850
or infrastructure
configurations that are

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.540
easy to share with your team.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:31.950
This gives your team a
convenient, secure, and

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.110
discoverable starting
point that adheres

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:37.350
to your organization's tech
stack and compliance needs.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.110
Custom samples also
encourage best practices

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.150
at the beginning of
your development cycle

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:46.110
and helps accelerate the time
to market for your team's ideas.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.030
To get started with
custom samples,

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.910
your organization first
needs to define applications

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.340
and services in a
version to Git repo that

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.370
includes their techniques for
improving security, compliance,

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:58.360
and other requirements.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.440
Once configured, this is
called a custom samples repo.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.080
Developers with permission
to the custom samples repo

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.930
can then use Cloud Code to
connect to your organization's

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.750
custom samples repo,
letting them easily import

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.970
individual apps and services.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.270
That way, your developers spend
more time coding and less time

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.630
configuring apps to meet your
organization's requirements.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.540
Cloud Code is available
for a variety of IDEs,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:25.890
including Jet Brands IDEs,
VSCode, and Cloud Shell Editor.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.320
For this video, I'll
demo custom samples

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.000
with Visual Studio code.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.670
I already have Cloud Code
installed from the VSCode

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.180
extensions marketplace.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.500
So I'm ready to go.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.470
Let's say, my
engineering manager

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.830
has set up a custom samples
repo of starter applications

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:42.690
that my team frequently uses.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.990
All these apps can easily
be imported into my IDE's

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.360
Workspace with Cloud Code.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.100
To show what this
flow looks like,

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.490
I'll use an example Git repo
the Cloud Code team made.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:56.910
What makes this Git repo
a custom samples repo is

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:59.120
the inclusion of one file--

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.140
.cctemplate.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.670
They look something like this.

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.830
Every samples
folder in your repo

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.200
needs to be referenced
in this file.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.470
But let's go back to importing
those custom samples repo.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:14.610
First, I'll grab the
Git URL for the repo.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.920
Switching back over
to VSCode, I can

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.650
go through the same flow
of creating a sample app.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.830
But instead of selecting a
Kubernetes or Cloud Run app,

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.860
I'll instead click
Custom Application.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.900
Then, I'll click Import
Sample from repo and paste

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.250
in the URL of the repo.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.160
All the samples from
that repo are now

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.610
ready to be individually
imported into my Workspace.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.710
And now, I'm ready to
code up a surface that

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:46.010
has all the dependencies
I need already included.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:47.750
If you want to set
up a custom samples

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:49.880
repo for your
organization, you can

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.220
fork our example,
custom samples repo,

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:55.380
and customize it to meet
your organization's needs.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.070
If you have any questions or
feature requests about Cloud

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.140
Code, join our Slack channel.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:02.350
Until next time.

